Token expiry checks are wrong on any server not running in UTC. Save /workspace/tokens.py:

from datetime import datetime

def make_token(payload, lifetime_seconds):
    return {"payload": payload, "exp": datetime.utcnow().timestamp() + lifetime_seconds}

def is_valid(token):
    return datetime.utcnow().timestamp() < token["exp"]

datetime.utcnow() returns a NAIVE datetime; .timestamp() then interprets it as LOCAL time, so exp is shifted by the machine's UTC offset. Reproduce in tests/test_tokens.py: set os.environ['TZ'] = 'America/Los_Angeles' and call time.tzset(), then assert make_token({}, 3600)['exp'] is within 5 seconds of time.time() + 3600. Run pytest and show the multi-hour skew. Fix by using datetime.now(timezone.utc) or plain time.time(), then parametrize the test over TZ values America/Los_Angeles, UTC, and Asia/Kolkata with a fixture that calls tzset and restores the previous TZ afterward. Also give is_valid boundary tests by monkeypatching the clock instead of sleeping. All green.

